A||||4||Fiasco||Windage tray good idea?||||5||
Z||000000||Fiasco||04-14-2000||10:39 PM||||Just wondering if the V6 windage tray is a good idea for average "get on it once in awhile" driving or if it is mainly for race set-ups.<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||04-14-2000||11:07 PM||||I don't think the 2.8 V6 really needs one. I'm pretty sure those windage trays are made for motors running around 8,000 RPMs. I could be wrong though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-15-2000||01:09 AM||||The windage tray is beneficial if you race on a road course our take curves at high speed. (highway exit ramps)The Fiero in a hard right hand turn can get oil starved. <BR>The windage tray helps keep the oil near the crankshaft journals to keep the main and rod bearings lubricated.<BR>There is also a piece that sticks down to help hold the oil near the pick-up tube during cornering so the pick-up tube does not suck air. Is it worth the cost and time to put one in is up to you and your driving habits. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||04-15-2000||01:16 AM||||I ahve taken my Fiero on SCCA rallies, gone acer a hundred a twenty and have nevr needed to install a windage tray. The only time they are useful is if your engine is doing over 8ooo rpm's. I am not the only one who says do not install one. Waste of time and money. Ed Parks will not install them because he doesn't beleive in them and neither do I. Some people have installed them and say they are good. But they are just saying what they heard from other people and don't have a clue if it works or not but believe that it does.<BR>Why do they think it does is because somebody told them it did but if you ask them who they don't remember.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 04-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiasco||04-15-2000||01:30 AM||||Thanks, guys, for the info.<BR>I think I'll take your word for it and skip the tray. (more money for beer)... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
